{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And he called the name of the well Esek.</b> Scripture tells the story and elaborates at length concerning the matter of the wells, yet in the plain sense of the narrative there is no benefit nor great honor for Isaac, for he and his father did the same thing. But there is a hidden matter within it, for it comes to inform of a future thing: for \"a well of living water\" alludes to the House of Elohim that the children of Isaac will build, and therefore it mentions \"a well of living waters,\" as it says (Jeremiah 17:13), \"the fountain of living waters, YHWH.\" And he called the first one Esek — it alludes to the First House, concerning which they strove with us and made against us many disputes and many wars until they destroyed it. And the second he called Sitnah — a harsher name than the first — and it is the Second House, which he called by its very name, as it is written concerning it (Ezra 4:6), \"And in the reign of Ahasuerus, at the beginning of his reign, they wrote an accusation against the inhabitants of Judah and Jerusalem,\" and all its days they were to us for hatred until they destroyed it and we were exiled from it in a bitter exile. And the third he called Rehobot — it is the future House that will be built swiftly in our days, and it will be made without strife and contention, and El will broaden our border, as it is said (Deuteronomy 19:8), \"And if YHWH your Elohim broadens your border, as He has spoken,\" etc., which refers to the future, and it is written of the Third House (Ezekiel 41:7), \"And it broadened and went round about upward and upward,\" and we shall be fruitful in the land — that all the nations shall serve Him with one accord."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:35:35.983039149Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}